The board is valid under gravity with pieces stacked from the bottom (row 5). Columns 2 and 4 are already full, so the legal drops for \(x\) are in columns 0, 1, 3, 5 and 6.

No drop by \(x\) produces four-in-a-row:
- (1,0) yields at most two consecutive \(x\);
- (2,1) yields at most two consecutive \(x\);
- (0,3) yields at most two consecutive \(x\);
- (5,5) and (5,6) are isolated from existing \(x\) groups.

Opponent \(o\) has an immediate win if allowed to drop in column 1 (landing at (2,1)). That single stone simultaneously completes the horizontal four \(oooo\) in row 2 (columns 1-4) and the diagonal four (2,1)-(3,2)-(4,3)-(5,4). No other legal cell gives \(o\) four-in-a-row.

Therefore the only move that prevents an instant loss is the block in column 1.

Final Answer: 1

